<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A launchd LaunchAgent, and a template rather than a finished file.

  `scripts/atrium-service.sh install` substitutes the five @PLACEHOLDERS@ and
  writes the result to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.dovholuknf.atrium.plist.
  It is a template because a plist cannot expand a variable: launchd reads it as
  data, so every path in it has to be absolute and already correct, and there is
  no equivalent of systemd's %h.

  A LaunchAGENT, NOT A LaunchDAEMON, and this is the decision to read before
  changing anything here.

  A LaunchDaemon runs as root in the system context before anybody logs in. It
  has none of your PATH, your shell configuration, your ssh agent, your keychain
  or your Claude Code configuration, and every runner it started would inherit
  none of them. Supervision is most of what atrium is for, so a LaunchDaemon
  installs a version of atrium with its main feature missing and nothing to say
  so.

  It is the same decision `packaging/atrium.service` makes on Linux in being a
  user unit, and the same one `scripts/atrium-autostart.ps1` makes on Windows in
  being a logon task rather than a service. The three are one design on three
  platforms and should be changed together.

  The trade, stated rather than hidden: a LaunchAgent belongs to your GUI login
  session and goes away when you log out. macOS has no equivalent of
  `loginctl enable-linger`. The nearest thing is a LaunchDaemon, which is the
  thing this file exists not to be.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>io.github.dovholuknf.atrium</string>

  <!--
    STARTED THROUGH A LOGIN SHELL, ON PURPOSE, and this is the part most likely
    to look like an unnecessary indirection.

    A LaunchAgent does not inherit your shell's environment. It gets the PATH
    launchd was configured with, which is /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin plus
    whatever /etc/paths.d holds. `claude` is usually in none of those: it lands
    in ~/.local/bin, or a node prefix, or Homebrew. A daemon that starts fine
    and then cannot spawn a single runner is the failure this avoids, and it is
    a failure that looks like atrium being broken rather than like a PATH.

    `-l` reads the login files, which is where PATH is set on a machine that
    works at all. `exec` so that launchd supervises atrium itself and not a
    shell holding it, which matters for the SIGTERM below: without it the
    signal reaches the shell and atrium is killed rather than wound down.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@SHELL@</string>
    <string>-l</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>exec "@EXE@" daemon --db "@DB@"</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    RESTARTED ON FAILURE, NOT UNCONDITIONALLY.

    A bare <true/> here means `launchctl stop` starts it straight back up, and
    `atrium stop` becomes a command that appears to do nothing. SuccessfulExit
    false says: bring it back when it died badly, leave it down when it exited
    cleanly, which is what a wind-down is. It matches Restart=on-failure in the
    systemd unit.
  -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>

  <!--
    Ten seconds, matching the daemon's own grace period for supervised runners
    plus room for the listeners. launchd sends SIGTERM, waits this long, then
    sends SIGKILL. Too short and a stop becomes a kill, which takes every
    supervised runner down at the moment atrium was trying to wind them up.
  -->
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <!--
    Not throttled. The default process type lets macOS deprioritise a process it
    decides is background work, and a terminal a person is typing into is not
    that. Interactive keeps the ptys responsive.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Interactive</string>

  <!--
    launchd has no journal. Without these two, everything the daemon prints goes
    nowhere and the first sign of a problem is a board that is not there.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>@LOGDIR@/atrium.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@LOGDIR@/atrium.log</string>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>@HOME@</string>
</dict>
</plist>
